WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.805
Safe path construction combines a trusted
base directory with a validated relative

2
00:00:04.885 --> 00:00:05.188
name.

3
00:00:05.268 --> 00:00:07.027
The root defines the allowed area.

4
00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:09.411
The name selects a resource inside that
area.

5
00:00:09.491 --> 00:00:13.735
The join operation should preserve that
relationship and should fail clearly when

6
00:00:13.815 --> 00:00:15.877
the candidate does not fit the workflow.

7
00:00:15.957 --> 00:00:18.018
Ad hoc string concatenation is fragile.

8
00:00:18.098 --> 00:00:21.918
It can mishandle separators, duplicate
separators, missing separators,

9
00:00:21.998 --> 00:00:25.152
length limits, platform conventions, or
buffer boundaries.

10
00:00:25.232 --> 00:00:29.415
In C, it can also introduce ordinary
memory safety mistakes if buffers and lengths

11
00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:30.890
are not handled carefully.

12
00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:35.214
Prefer small, reviewed helpers that
centralize path construction and enforce the

13
00:00:35.294 --> 00:00:36.628
same rules consistently.

14
00:00:36.708 --> 00:00:41.135
Normalization can simplify path text, but
normalized text is not automatically safe.

15
00:00:41.215 --> 00:00:44.489
It may reduce redundant pieces or
platform-specific spelling,

16
00:00:44.569 --> 00:00:47.783
yet the resulting path still needs to be
judged against policy.

17
00:00:47.863 --> 00:00:51.622
Normalization is a tool for clarity, not a
permission decision by itself.

18
00:00:51.702 --> 00:00:55.886
Canonicalization or resolution can help
when the program needs to compare actual

19
00:00:55.966 --> 00:00:56.572
locations.

20
00:00:56.652 --> 00:01:00.897
For example, a candidate can be resolved
and then compared with the approved root to

21
00:01:00.977 --> 00:01:04.312
confirm that the final location remains
inside the managed area.

22
00:01:04.392 --> 00:01:06.453
That comparison must be done carefully,

23
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:10.656
with attention to platform behavior and
the possibility that the file system can

24
00:01:10.736 --> 00:01:11.161
change.

25
00:01:11.241 --> 00:01:14.273
Path construction should have explicit
failure behavior.

26
00:01:14.353 --> 00:01:17.566
If a name is invalid, a path is too long,
a resolution step fails,

27
00:01:17.646 --> 00:01:20.436
or the resolved location is outside the
approved area,

28
00:01:20.516 --> 00:01:24.154
the operation should fail cleanly before
any file is opened or changed.

29
00:01:24.234 --> 00:01:28.114
The caller should receive a clear result
that does not require guessing what

30
00:01:28.194 --> 00:01:28.740
happened.
